      "text": "To solve the problem, let's start by expressing the given expression in a more manageable form. The expression is:\n\n\\[ (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) \\]\n\nFirst, observe that both \\(10x + 2\\) and \\(10x + 6\\) can be factored:\n\n\\[ 10x + 2 = 2(5x + 1) \\]\n\\[ 10x + 6 = 2(5x + 3) \\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[ (2(5x + 1))(2(5x + 3))(5x + 5) = 4(5x + 1)(5x + 3)(5x + 5) \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the largest integer that always divides this expression for any odd integer \\(x\\). Let's simplify the product \\( (5x + 1)(5x + 3)(5x + 5) \\).\n\nNotice that \\(5x + 1\\), \\(5x + 3\\), and \\(5x + 5\\) are three consecutive integers. Among any three consecutive integers, one of them is divisible by 2 and at least one is divisible by 3. Additionally, at least one of these three integers will be divisible by 3 (since 5x can be any integer, and when we add 1, 3, and 5, two of these will always be multiples of 3).\n\nThus, the product \\( (5x + 1)(5x + 3)(5x + 5) \\) is always divisible by \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 30\\).\n\nNow, let's multiply by 4:\n\n\\[ 4 \\times 30 = 120 \\]\n\nSo, the expression \\( (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) \\) is always divisible by 120. To confirm, we can check a few values of \\(x\\) to ensure this is always true.\n\nFor \\(x = 1\\):\n\n\\[ (10 \\cdot 1 + 2)(10 \\cdot 1 + 6)(5 \\cdot 1 + 5) = 12 \\cdot 16 \\cdot 10 = 1920 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Divisible by } 120 \\]\n\nFor \\(x = 3\\):\n\n\\[ (10 \\cdot 3 + 2)(10 \\cdot 3 + 6)(5 \\cdot 3 + 5) = 32 \\cdot 36 \\cdot 20 = 23040 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Divisible by } 120 \\]\n\nFor \\(x = -1\\):\n\n\\[ (10 \\cdot (-1) + 2)(10 \\cdot (-1) + 6)(5 \\cdot (-1) + 5) = (-8)(-4)(0) = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Divisible by } 120 \\]\n\nIt holds for all odd values of \\(x\\). Therefore, the largest integer that always divides the expression is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{120} \\]",
