      "text": "Let \\( x \\) be an odd number. We need to find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\( (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) \\).\n\nFirst, simplify the expression:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = (2(5x+1))(2(5x+3))(5(x+1)) = 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) = 16 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to analyze the divisibility of \\( 16 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\). Notice that \\( x \\) is odd, so \\( x = 2k + 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). \n\nConsider each term in the product:\n1. \\( 16 = 2^4 \\)\n2. \\( 5x + 1 = 5(2k + 1) + 1 = 10k + 6 = 2(5k + 3) \\)\n3. \\( 5x + 3 = 5(2k + 1) + 3 = 10k + 8 = 4(5k + 2) \\)\n4. \\( x + 1 = 2k + 1 + 1 = 2k + 2 = 2(2k + 1) \\)\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n16 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) = 16 \\cdot (2(5k + 3))(4(5k + 2))(2(2k + 1))\n\\]\n\\[\n= 16 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (5k + 3)(5k + 2)(2k + 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 64 \\cdot (5k + 3)(5k + 2)(2k + 1)\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 5k + 3 \\), \\( 5k + 2 \\), and \\( 2k + 1 \\) are all integers, their product is divisible by \\( 2^4 = 16 \\). Therefore, the expression \\( 16 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\) is always divisible by \\( 16 \\).\n\nTo check if a larger integer divides the expression, we need to check the divisibility of the product by \\( 2^5 = 32 \\). We need to check if \\( 10x+2 \\), \\( 10x+6 \\), and \\( 5x+5 \\) are divisible by \\( 2^5 = 32 \\).\n\nFor \\( 10x + 2 \\):\n\\[\n10x + 2 = 2(5x + 1)\n\\]\nSince \\( x \\) is odd, \\( 5x + 1 \\) is even, so \\( 10x + 2 \\) is divisible by \\( 2^2 = 4 \\) but not by \\( 2^5 = 32 \\).\n\nFor \\( 10x + 6 \\):\n\\[\n10x + 6 = 2(5x + 3)\n\\]\nSince \\( x \\) is odd, \\( 5x + 3 \\) is even, so \\( 10x + 6 \\) is divisible by \\( 2^2 = 4 \\) but not by \\( 2^5 = 32 \\).\n\nFor \\( 5x + 5 \\):\n\\[\n5x + 5 = 5(x + 1)\n\\]\nSince \\( x \\) is odd, \\( x + 1 \\) is even, so \\( 5x + 5 \\) is divisible by \\( 2 \\) but not by \\( 2^5 = 32 \\).\n\nSince none of the factors \\( 10x + 2 \\), \\( 10x + 6 \\), or \\( 5x + 5 \\) are divisible by \\( 2^5 = 32 \\), the largest integer that always divides the expression \\( (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) \\) is \\( 16 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{32}\n\\]",
